Time as a concept alone is a difficult thing to tackle. We live in a system that forces us to oblige time, or at least the clock. Clocks as a concept have not been around for very long on the human timeline. There have been many attempts throughout history to measure the way the earth and the sun move, with many combinations thereof and beyond. Sundials, obelisks, and ancient calendars informed by the movements of the stars come to mind. Though it hasn’t existed for as long as these other modes of measurement, the modern clock was made to drive people into a system of control they are bound and indentured to.

As a concept, the earliest standardization of time came from religious institutions. In order for monks to keep track of their daily responsibilities and activities, a way to keep time was a necessity. Some of the first clocks were invented by Christian monks in Europe expressly for this purpose.

In the modern era, the concept of time discipline was created in order to give order to social conventions. Using time discipline, people have an expectation of one another’s actions based on common “times” for meals, sleep, or relaxation. The first person to give attention to this concept was E.P. Thompson, a sociologist and anthropologist who used Marxism as the starting point for his observations.

Essentially, Thompson’s argument boils down to the idea that capitalism as a system would not have been possible without the introduction:imposition of synchronized clocks from a standardized time zone system. Both governments and the so-called elite they serve worked in tandem to create a means of exploiting common people by finding ways to extract value from labor beyond just the labor itself. Time discipline made surplus value and the excess capitalism wants most so that those limited people with power can find and control more with that power. The earliest labor movements in industrialist society were fought over time and the expectations of the laboring class. Much blood was spilled in these conflicts, proving Thompson’s ideas and Marxist theory right in the sense that control through time discipline was needed in order to have a “working” capitalist system.

From a more scientific viewpoint, time is a dimension of reality that demands measurement based on the other dimensions that precede it. It’s a common trope to hear about how “time is the fourth dimension,” but is it something that many of us understand? On a fundamental level alone, i think we may only apply this idea to the time discipline we understand based on the system we live in. We know that right now is the present, right then was the past, and this bit coming up is the future. As such time is understood as a linear dimension, one that operates in a single line that moves forward only. It’s all we’re expected to understand so that we can make sure to wake up, get ready, beat traffic, and clock in on time. The other reasons we use time are secondary or worse in capitalism.

In order to make our way into the fourth dimension and deeper understandings of it and time as a whole, it’s best to understand the first three dimensions that lead us to (using) it.

The First Dimension: since it’s first, this would seem the most simple to comprehend, right? Perhaps! But how often do you truly think in one dimension? Have you ever challenged yourself to? The simplest way i’ve come up with to understand one dimensional space is by envisioning a single black dot in a white void. The dot is stationary, unable to move in any direction because it alone is the only thing that exists in this imaginary white void. No matter which way you approach the dot to look at it, it will be exactly the same from every angle. Despite the way this dot looks though, it can move along a single axis, the X axis. Whether that’s forward:backward, left:right, or whatever you:your perception chooses the dot can only move this one way. Still, the movement will be imperceptible since there are no other objects to measure the movement against in the white void. On this level, it can be understood that the X axis is one of horizontal movement.

The Second Dimension: We have our black dot, where can it go now that we add in a new dimension? The Y axis gives the dot the ability to move vertically in space. Our space of the white void isn’t very useful to us anymore. So instead, let’s use something that most of us will have some experience with whether hands on or at least in concept: old school video games. Specifically, let’s use Super Mario Bros. For those who don’t know, Super Mario Bros. is widely considered one of the best video games of its generation. Credited with aiding the comeback of video games as an industry after a massive oversaturation caused a collapse, this game has you playing as Mario, an Italian plumber from Brooklyn, New York who falls down a pipe one day and winds up in an entirely new world called the Mushroom Kingdom. Mario is a two dimensional, or 2D, game. Mario himself can move along both the X and Y axises. He can run horizontally across the screen both left and right (again, our perception being left and right as forced by the television screen in front of us) AND jump:climb:traverse vertically over blocks, pits, enemies, and hazards of all kinds. Watching Mario move gives us an immediate understanding of two dimensional space and movement. Controlling him gives us hands-on experience with two dimensions.

The Third Dimension: By adding the Z axis, we finally arrive at our daily experience. We, as in not just humanity but all living animals no matter where they live on the planet, experience the world around us using principles of three dimensional space such as depth, circumference, and the means by which we gauge how we move through our world:space. It is important to note however that our perception of three dimensional space is still ruled by two dimensional objects, though. Technically speaking, eyes give us a flat image of what’s directly in front of us at any given time. It is the way our brains process this information that gives us an understanding of the Z axis. Our environment does exist in three dimensions, but it’s up to each of us:our abilities to give this dimension the meaning by which we traverse it.

Alright. Now that we have all of this under our proverbial belts, we can tackle what it 

means for time to be the fourth dimension. Let’s break down 1:2:3 to single ideas. X, Y, Z. Horizontal, Vertical, Depth. Got it? Now try to imagine time as a single point in just the same way we imagined our black dot. Time, the Fourth Dimension, is also a linear dimension. We are only expected to understand it in this way, as a single point by which we gauge our lives. Our work schedules are fourth dimensional, sure. But it’s time as a single line. Past, Present, Future. See? Linear.

A common cliche in spiritual contexts you’ve no doubt ALSO heard before is how all we truly have is the present. Think about it: do you have access to your past in a tangible:physical way? Maybe through trinkets you’ve kept for sentimental value. But can YOU, the person reading this, return to your past in a real way right this second? No, you can’t (but if you can, let me know. And don’t send me the Time Cube website, i raised myself using the internet and know more about all that than you do haha). Better yet, can you move into the future in any way aside from linearly, letting the tick of the clock beat on? No, you can’t. The future doesn’t yet physically exist. As a concept, sure. The future is a place we all can go in our minds and we often do. Some of us call it anxiety. Others, daydreaming. No matter what though, we’re not moving forward in time in any meaningful way aside from one second at a time, and even then the “future” immediately becomes the present. 

So how do we make sense of anything in our experience? The ways we’ve been taught to manage our lives is how we perceive our place in the world. How well most of us manage it, at least initially, is a combination of both nature and nurture. Nature in the sense that humanity as a whole has passed down this information via DNA through the ages. We make each decision on CI (the bio-survival circuit) based on this information exclusively. We move away from things that threaten our survival and move toward things that nourish us. Nurture is in the ways we’ve been raised by the people in our lives. Caregivers can only show us how best they know how to manage their lives through the life experience they pass down to us when we interact. We’re encouraged through education (either given:imposed by caregivers, from our school systems, or self-directed interest) to seek:take in other answers depending on how beneficial we feel this education can be on the fundamental CI level.

This deeper understanding is aided by the experiences we have with a focus on CII (the emotional-territorial circuit). Part of the understanding of the world we gain from caregivers:education:ourselves is how to manage our emotional states. Just as in matters of CI, we are often made to have a predisposition to certain emotional states based on our biology and on the ways we are raised. Caregivers have a stake in your emotional well being. Essentially they don’t want you to be a living terror that overtakes their lives and their homes. The easiest way to do that is to make you like them because at least that way they know how to manage you (insofar as they are able to manage themselves). At the very least it will be familiar to them, and so they can approach you in a similar way.

Territorial management is made first at home with our caregivers and then made and made and made again through our various experiences with both our peers and those of higher “ranks.” To reiterate the point made in CII, our physicality, perception of gender, our dispositions (remember our areas of Strength:Weakness?) and now our own “rank” as determined by the prevailing social order determines where we end up in our territory. The territory we occupy determines the map our mind makes. For most of us, what we perceive is what we believe. If we’re made to feel lesser, without proper education (mainly from our caregivers), it’s difficult for us to move out of a mindset of being lesser [it is my hope that some of the information in this work can help you move away from such a mindset, but i digress]. 

	How then do we even process all of this incoming information? From the moment we’re born whether we realize it or not, we’re taking in the world around us not only through the physicality and perceived body language attitudes of the society we’re born into but also the language our society speaks. All those fun clicky, hummy, burbling noises we can’t yet understand are being processed by our minds:brains and some sense of order can only be made of any of it through education. Learning to read and write is more than just how we manage our existence in the world we live in. Language is a way to educate and as a result is a way to indoctrinate. The two are intrinsically linked. You can’t have one without the other. Education is impure. There are always agendas to the things we’re taught. Whether it’s a way to respect our caregivers or to be a good little cog in the vast machines of our societies, we’re only taught to understand language that we might understand anything, and by understanding things we’re made aware of how we can serve things such as interests both personal and common.

RAW in PR refers to languages in a more basic way with a broader meaning as human symbol systems. “These systems include words, artworks, music, rituals and unrecognized rituals (‘games’) through which culture is transmitted,” [underlined emphasis mine]. Of course in their own ways, each of these symbol systems are languages unto themselves. They are ways of understanding our world largely by means of play. To play is essential to understanding, to understand is to navigate, and to navigate is to live. One leads to the other in this linear fashion, but allows us the ability to move beyond a single means by which to understand anything.

Further, understanding leads naturally into categorization. To categorize our experiences in smaller, more digestible ways means we can quickly discern the things that are most important to us in any given moment. When we’re able to assess things fast enough, we’re able to react in the ways that may serve us best in any given situation. This doesn’t apply only to immediate needs that stress us. It’s just as applicable to choosing what coffee you want at the cafe as it is to avoiding a car accident when you’re behind the wheel. Though it makes our individual realities more digestible, this categorization makes it far too easy to either reinforce or create biases. 

A bias is just a predisposition for a way of thinking in the same way our brains are predisposed to ways of thinking by nature:nurture. Make no mistake: all of us have biases ingrained in us. Our first biases are our caregiver’s biases. This is an essential function of human symbol systems. A bias transmitted via a symbol system leads to education:indoctrination. The two forms of digestion are indistinguishable at a young age. Without the context of a greater world around us, none of us are immune to propaganda. This is how some of the most heinous of ideas such as racism, sexism, and phobias of all kinds are propagated throughout history:the world.

Specifically when it comes to the meaning of the words we use to spread ideas, there is more to separate so that we can understand how meaning is spread through symbol systems. RAW gives us a great shorthand here: “...words contain both denotations (referents in the sensory-existential world) and connotations (emotional tones and poetic or rhetorical hooks)...” Denotations tell us about the people:objects we experience in our physical realities daily. Connotations give us a sense of how we or others feel about our physical realities BUT ALSO about the ideas and perceptions we have on an emotional level about that which has no physical basis in reality. Concepts and ideas are in our minds but we still know how we feel about them. It’s only through connotations that we’re able to make sense of those feelings.

Does this categorization of our realities ever stop? Not unless you are able to calm your mind through meditation, drugs, or other means (and frankly if you know of other means feel free to let me know about them). “On the personal level, this is the ‘internal monologue’…On the historical level, this is the time-binding function…which allows each generation to add new categories to our mental library,” RAW explains. Through storytelling, child rearing, and transmission via our physical selves through our DNA, we as a species have built a kind of master library for the totality of human experience as passed down through our lineages. Where else do ideas go but in books? Where else do books go but in libraries? It’s a system we’ve always used even before the symbols we developed to tell one another about our lives found their way onto the cave walls. Eventually we found a way to get our thoughts into a more physical form that we might share them. How is a library any different than the machinations of a mind?

And so, finally, we can see how these symbol systems transmit ideas through time and how CIII becomes the time-binding circuit by which we rule ourselves and each other. Ideas are passed down through the meanings of words. Even nonsense words like portmanteaus (words that combine two or more words to make a new word. ex - phonana, combining both phone and banana) or made up words like “synergy” carry meaning where they made no sense before. Each successive generation combines, splits apart, recategorizes, and ultimately changes language based on their observation and understanding of the world. And so symbol systems become the basis by which we are ruled. Explained in PR: “Human beings (domesticated primates) are symbol-using creatures; which means, as the pioneer semanticist, Korzybski, noted, that those who rule symbols, rule us.”

The basis for all of our ever present “culture wars” is based on the co-option, use, and thus meaning of our symbol systems. Since those who rule symbols rule us, those who thrust meaning onto our symbols (and thus whoever “gains ground” over the meaning of specific symbols into the most common understanding of said symbols in the collective consciousness) are the ones trying desperately to force their realities onto the rest of us. When there’s contention over the meanings of these symbols, there is a battle to be fought. Unfortunately, ideology and rhetoric is used liberally on every side of an argument and appeals to logic do not apply. 

Recall from CII: most people are unable to access a higher understanding or analysis of the words:symbols we use. Most are content to take the meanings they know:are told and be done with it. As a result, politicians gain favor over people using tactics that cause confusion, discomfort, and fear over their constituency. A politician shows they know the deeper meanings of these words:symbols but appeals to people in an almost animalistic:territorial way. Within a system built to cause fear, and most people unable to process this fear, any notable figure can become an authority if their word:symbol use causes an emotional reaction within people that they can’t or refuse to understand. The meaning becomes common, the common person takes in that meaning without questioning it, and rhetoric becomes ideology thus creating another lens over the eyes of those who do not question their world.

“...the semantic circuit is so easily perverted into false logics (bigotries, intolerant ideologies, fanaticisms of all sorts) when the bio-survival circuit [CI] signals threat to life or the emotional circuit [CII] flashes threat to status.” What RAW could never take into account because of the digital age is our new “reliance” on social media. He understood that “...all times are ‘times of change.’ The process is however accelerating faster as time passes, because the symbolizing faculty is self-augmenting.” In other words, we’re changing meaning faster and faster because of how easy it is to change it in a world that continuously increases the speed at which we communicate. In our time, meanings of words can change in as little as a day because of social media. Every post, tweet, and tossed out thought adds to the augmentation of our symbols and thus generations of people’s strongly held beliefs are thrown into question in nearly every context should they actively engage in the conversation.

“Whoever can scare people enough (produce bio-survival anxiety) can sell them quickly on any verbal map that seems to give them relief, i.e., cure the anxiety…These…mammalian reflexes are known, respectively, as Religion and Patriotism. They work for domesticated primates, as for the wild primates, because they are Evolutionary Relative Successes. (So far.)” Where RAW falls short of an interpretation for our modern world is his underestimation of cults of personality. Think of every person who follows Joe Rogan religiously, or any “celebrity”:popularized face in the media we consume. People have created entire ideologies based only on the words that come from their favorite personality of choice. The lack of any questioning as to the rationale of the logic presented by these cult figures is an ideal situation for those that espouse their beliefs on a global scale. Though these days is there any smaller scale? Many social media platforms are nearly ubiquitous (except in the places where they’re blocked by national governments) and even if the direct posts from these cult leaders aren’t available, the ideas can spread from person to person on every individual platform. [As an aside, the personal torch i carry is vehemently AGAINST Elon Musk. It has been my experience that people who buy his cars or follow his life are some of the most indoctrinated into certain lines of thinking. They tend to be self-righteous, pompous, overly assertive:aggressive, and see themselves as “better” than other people. Shoutout to the man who cut me off in the dual drive thru lines at McDonald’s for no reason, you know who you are.]

All this to say, the acceleration of ideas has reached a breaking point. Things change so quickly with such regularity that no one can reasonably be expected to keep up with “the times.” Those that try live their lives entirely in front of their favorite glowing rectangles (phones, laptops, PC’s, televisions). So consumed are they by these changes that they begin to regurgitate the ideas fed to them by these rectangles without a second thought. They aid acceleration without awareness of the consequences of their actions. Culture is now no more than the latest sound bite from some talking head who wants you to buy their products:worship them:accept their rationale.

From this acceleration of ideas due to modern day CULTure worship comes a kind of digital tribalism. If people are able to attach themselves to a leader without physical borders then they can wind up sharing a specific kind of groupthink. Social media has become a battlegrounds for ideology in much the same way the physical world is a battleground for resources and territory. Truly it’s not much different. In the same way we kill one another for places to live:exploit, we are attempting to kill the programming inside one another that we might conquer minds. Not only are we living in our own maps, we’re attempting to rewrite the maps of others.

The thing is, everyone’s reality is completely subjective. We can’t know what the lives of other people on the other side of the screen is TRULY like. We might wind up assuming much of the time that whoever we’re screaming at through screen:type has a similar mindset to us to begin with. We think others think like us so that we try to communicate to others in ways that WE understand. What so often happens is something is said, something is misunderstood, and then a “flame war” breaks out. Especially between “warring” ideologies other people on either side of the flames wind up involving themselves in the battle at hand. When people attach themselves to a way of thinking about the world, they find personal offense to any attacks on it and take up “arms” to defend it. This has been happening in digital spaces for as long as i can remember.

