When it comes to time, CI and CII only allow one understanding of it: in either the grip of fears induced by threat (CI) or the emotional turmoil we face when trying to make a decision about something important to us (CII) time stops. Panic and anxiety both cause our bodies to release adrenaline to prepare our nervous systems for fight, flight, fawn, or freeze. Whatever conscious awareness is available in either of these two states is minimal; often we find the decision we must make is imperative. Whether or not that’s true is up for interpretation but it’s true enough in our physiology that it reacts in this way.

In CIII, “...time becomes conceptualized as well as experienced.” To conceptualize time is to understand time in a greater context than the immediate world in front of our collective faces. CI and CII prevent this. CI is based on our immediate needs and as such time only exists in the seconds it takes for us to react to whatever threat needs addressing. CII changes our perception of time because being in a heightened emotional state is the result of being challenged in our personally held beliefs or dogmas.The existence of time in this emotional reaction is not important enough to make an impact on how we respond to this different, but still very real kind of threat. It may not be a threat to our physical well being in the same way a hungry wild cougar might be, but threats to our mindsets are threats all the same. Is it any wonder that ‘keyboard warriors’ are a VERY REAL phenomena, then? They cause themselves to be stuck in this physical:emotional condemnation of their well-being in ways that trigger their minds into a cycle that disturbs as much as satisfies (even if they’d never admit it). 
